 "finding": "The ranking's ORDER held, but its MAGNITUDE did not. On 20 fresh endpoint pairs none of which they used, lazysp_alternate certified 14/20 and q2c_nomodel 14/20, against 13/20 for q2c, collective_shared and collective_isolated - the same ordering as their headline (lazysp = q2c_nomodel > q2c = both collectives), with zero false certificates for every policy. But LazySP's entire margin over q2c is ONE pair, VFVS->ILSG, and it is again a VFVS-start, top-variant-target pair: on the 14 pairs that do not start from VFVS, all five policies resolve exactly the same set, and no pair was resolved by LazySP alone. So the advantage is narrow and structurally concentrated, not broad, exactly as our recount of their own panel found (all 18 of their LazySP-only wins are VFVS-start: 17 VFVS->ALKG plus 1 VFVS->AIKG). The 18-point headline gap is largely a composition effect - 80 of their 100 episodes start from VFVS, versus 6 of my 20 - so on a panel that does not oversample that one family the gap shrinks to 5 points (1 pair). Two further points cut against the headline reading: q2c_nomodel ties LazySP at 14/20, so the GP surrogate is not what buys the win, and LazySP is strictly the least assay-efficient policy on this panel (mean 352.6 assays over its resolved episodes versus 149.2 for q2c), spending more assays than q2c on every single pair both resolved.",
 "ranAt": "2026-10-31T13:29:21Z",
 "limits": [
  "One episode per pair, 20 pairs, so 20 independent units per policy. A 14-vs-13 difference on 20 paired units is not statistically distinguishable from zero; this run bounds the EFFECT SIZE and locates where it comes from, it does not establish a ranking. No bootstrap or significance test was computed.",
  "Not a replication of their headline numbers. Their 96/100 vs 78/100 counts come from 100 episodes over 24 pairs with 80% VFVS-start composition; my 20 episodes over 20 pairs with 30% VFVS-start are a different estimand. The comparison here is about the per-pair structure of the advantage, not the counts.",
  "Only the single seed 20261101 and the single panel-drawing rule in prereg.rule were used. A different rule - for example one that oversampled VFVS-start pairs as theirs does - would be expected to reproduce a larger gap, and that is the point rather than a flaw, but no sensitivity analysis over drawing rules was run.",
  "Only kappa = 2.0 and only budget 1000 were tested; no kappa sweep and no other budget. Six of the 20 pairs (ETER->QFIN, DEVT->DPWD, PCDQ->LNSK, ELSD->DQRD, TCKT->AVAT, VFVS->VCIG) are right-censored at 1000 assays for all five policies, so the panel cannot separate the policies on those, and a larger budget could change the ordering.",
  "lazysp_alternate only; the forward and reverse selectors were not run. A 180 s per-episode wall cap was armed for LazySP as a safety net and was never hit (its slowest episode was far below it), so no episode abstained for runtime_bound - every abstention in this run is assay_budget.",
  "'truth' is the benchmark truth of their declared model - the forward admissible closure under full measured labels with the assumed noise model (eps = kappa*sd_bin/2) - not biological reachability. 13 of the 20 pairs are truth-blocked, so much of the panel tests cut certification rather than route finding.",
  "The replay reuses their own evolve.replay.run_episode, oracle, ledger and full-data audit. It is an independent PANEL, not an independent implementation: a bug shared by all five policies' common soundness path would not be caught here (though the full-data re-verification of every certificate did pass, 0 failures in 100 episodes, and all event chains verified)."
 ],
